**router4 FRR config**: **completely empty — no routing protocol whatsoever**

This is the root cause. Here is the diagnosis:

---

```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router4
diagnosis_text:
Router4 is the first router out of the pc11/pc12/pc13/pc14 office site (subnet
192.168.200.0/24, reachable via switch4 → switch5/switch6). Its FRR daemon is
running but its running configuration is completely empty — no routing protocol
(RIP, OSPF, or BGP) is configured. Every other router in this topology
participates in RIP version 2; router3 (router4's upstream neighbor via
link_r4_r3, 172.25.1.0/24) is configured to listen for RIP on 172.25.0.0/16,
which covers that very link, so it would automatically learn 192.168.200.0/24
if router4 were advertising it. Because router4 is silent on RIP, none of the
other routers (router0–router3, router5, router6) hold any route to
192.168.200.0/24. Router4 does have a kernel-level static default route pointing
to router3 (172.25.1.2), so outbound packets from pc11–14 leave the site and
reach the rest of the network. However, every router along the return path drops
the reply (no route to 192.168.200.0/24), so 100% of inter-site traffic silently
fails. Local gateway reachability is intact (pc11 can ping 192.168.200.1 via L2),
but any destination beyond the subnet is unreachable, which users experience as
complete site isolation. Root cause: RIP is missing from router4's FRR
configuration. Fix: add a RIP stanza to router4 advertising its connected
networks — at minimum `network 192.168.200.0/24`, `network 172.25.1.0/24`, and
`network 192.168.15.0/24`, with `version 2` — so router3 and router5 learn the
prefix and propagate it throughout the RIP domain.
=== END ===
```